t concept you need to  know what you need to study and what you  need to learn if you try to learn  everything that's presented to you  incredibly deeply I don't think you'd  ever be able to pass because there's  just so much to do you need to be  thinking in terms of I guess how can I  be most effective at doing some of these  example problems but not every single  one so you have to be able to look for  like common trends and what you're being  asked and pick out trends with within  problems and ideas and and just learn  those it's I think almost impossible to  try to do and accomplish every concept  and every problem so to the Instagram  comments there's one what is your  specialization in studying physics so in  my undergrad degree that I did in New  Zealand I double majored in physics and  maths and I didn't specialize beyond  that I know some unis offer you  especially the beginnings with a lot of  courses offer specializations like  Medical Physics theoretical physics  maybe optics something like that and  they offer that at the undergrad level I  just had that sort of board physics  umbrella even though I did a lot of say  astrophysics courses and even some of  the more mathematical physics I don't  really have a label now that I'm doing  my PhD I'm working in an area of  biophysics but it's still just under the  umbrella  PhD in physics direct notation and also  how many females study physics so I have  made a video talking about direct  notation before and I'll try and put a  link to that here but the Sigma part of  the question how many female said in  physics the answer is not many which is  a shame and there's definitely not many  I probably say in my undergrad course I  was at quite a small uni and the whole  class like all the physics majors it was  probably 30 people in total and of those  maybe three or four girls so maybe a 10%  yeah it's really a shame and it does  make it harder I think for women doing  physics to not be surrounded by sort of  li